\f1\b \cf0 Letter to Clara\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0 \cf0 Clara,\
If you\'92re reading this, it means the space relay actually worked, which already puts this letter ahead of at least half of my past attempts at communication.\
I know it\'92s been a long time since we talked \'97 really talked, not just exchanged clipped holiday emails like strangers pretending to be cordial. And yes, the irony is not lost on me that it took me being stranded on a lifeless asteroid to finally write you something this long and honest. If you were here, you\'92d probably say, \'93Well, you finally ran out of excuses \'97 and Wi-Fi distractions.\'94 You\'92d be right.\
It\'92s been 47 days since I landed here on Nemo. Forty-seven days of dust, protein bars, a vending machine that occasionally groans like it\'92s haunted, and one rock I\'92ve named Gary. (Don\'92t roll your eyes. You\'92d like Gary. He\'92s reliable, which is more than I can say for the habitat heater.)\
When you left \'97 or maybe I left, depending on how you measure the breaking point \'97 I told myself we were just \'93different people now.\'94 That was my tidy little phrase, like a comic cutting to commercial after a bad punchline. The truth is, I didn\'92t fight for us. I didn\'92t even try. I think I was afraid of being told the fight wasn\'92t worth it.\
You used to tell me I lived for the laugh, not the moment. And you weren\'92t wrong. Comedy was my way of dodging anything that hurt too much. I could turn every argument into a joke before it landed, every disappointment into a bit. I was more comfortable with strangers clapping for a set I\'92d done a hundred times than I was sitting across from you at the kitchen table, asking what you needed from me.\
Out here, there\'92s no audience. Not a real one, anyway. Just Gary, the vending machine, and an AI with the sense of humor of a tax return. And without the laugh track, I\'92ve had to sit with the quiet. I\'92ve had to sit with myself.\
Do you remember that night we drove to the coast in December, just to watch the waves crash in the freezing dark? We barely spoke, but it felt like we were tuned to the same frequency. I\'92ve been chasing that feeling lately \'97 not the silence of space, but the kind of quiet that comes from knowing someone is right there beside you, even if neither of you says a word.\
I didn\'92t tell you enough that I loved you. And when I did say it, I often buried it inside some joke, like if I kept it casual it wouldn\'92t feel so vulnerable. But I did. I do. In a way that doesn\'92t fade just because we\'92re on different planets \'97 literally, now.\
You once asked me if I\'92d ever take a \'93real risk\'94 for something that mattered. I guess being here isn\'92t really that \'97 it\'92s more of a logistical accident than a noble gesture. But maybe this letter is my risk. Maybe telling you all of this with no punchline at the end is braver than anything I\'92ve done on stage.\
I don\'92t expect you to respond. I\'92m not writing this to win you back or to rewrite history from an asteroid. I just wanted you to know that if I make it home, I\'92m going to try to live a little more in the moment. To let people in before I deflect with a joke. To actually ask someone how they are, and wait for the answer.\
I hope you\'92re happy, Clara. Really happy \'97 the kind of happy where you don\'92t even notice the days passing because they\'92re so full. I hope you\'92ve found someone who loves all the quiet parts of you, and who lets you love theirs in return.\
If you ever do want to talk \'97 whether it\'92s in a caf\'e9 or a coast at midnight \'97 I\'92ll be there. Not the version of me who hides behind timing and wordplay, but me. Just me.\
For what it\'92s worth, you were always my best story.\
\'96 A.\
P.S. If you 
\f2\i do
\f0\i0  respond, please send coffee. Real coffee.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
