\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 Letter of Gratitude \'96 To the Crew of the SS Relentless Hope\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Dear Crew,\
When I first saw your ship in the sky above Nemo, it looked like a trick of the light. I had convinced myself, over those long months alone, that rescue was a myth \'97 a bedtime story I told myself in the dust and silence so I could sleep without thinking too much about how many sunrises I had left.\
And then you arrived.\
You arrived not like a fairy tale or a sudden miracle, but like something sturdy and real. Steel and fuel and calculation. Humanity, compressed into a vessel. You came not because it was easy, or glamorous, but because you had decided, somewhere along the way, that it mattered if I came home.\
You will never know what that moment felt like from the ground.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 In my months on Nemo, my world had shrunk to a handful of routines. Wake. Eat. Check the habitat\'92s readings. Talk to Gary (a rock, yes, but a good listener). Perform little ceremonies just to give shape to the days. There was no applause, no validation \'97 only the quiet rhythm of survival.\
I adapted. I learned to laugh alone. I learned to make a home out of something that was never meant to hold a person\'92s heart.\
But I also learned what absence feels like when it becomes the default state. I learned what it means to go weeks without another human voice in the room. I learned how loneliness can become so familiar that you stop noticing it\'92s there.\
And then I saw your ship.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 I want to thank you, first, for breaking the silence.\
When your voice came over the comm, I almost didn\'92t answer. I thought it was my mind playing another trick. But there you were \'97 not a recording, not an echo, but living, breathing people asking me if I was alright. I didn\'92t know what to say. How do you compress months of dust and hope and fear into a single reply? I think I just said, 
\f2\i \'93Do you have pizza?\'94
\f0\i0 Which, honestly, was the most honest thing I could have said.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Thank you for making the landing gentle.\
I didn\'92t know how I\'92d feel when the rescue actually happened. I expected panic. I expected awkwardness. I expected to feel like an alien stepping onto your deck. Instead, the first thing I felt was relief \'97 the kind you can only feel when someone else has taken the wheel and you\'92re allowed, finally, to stop gripping the armrests of your own survival.\
You didn\'92t rush me. You didn\'92t laugh at me when I insisted on giving you a tour of Nemo\'92s \'93historical sites\'94 (the word \'93site\'94 doing a lot of heavy lifting there). You let me say goodbye in my own way. That mattered more than I can tell you.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Thank you for treating me like a person, not a problem to be solved.\
I know it would have been easy to reduce me to \'93the guy we pulled off the rock.\'94 A name in a mission log. A checklist item. But you\'92ve treated me like a crewmate since I stepped aboard. You\'92ve invited me to meals. You\'92ve asked for stories \'97 even the weird ones. You\'92ve let me fail at making coffee in zero-g without writing it into the official record.\
You\'92ve given me laughter back. And you probably don\'92t realize how heavy it is to go without laughter for that long until you hear it again and remember that it doesn\'92t have to echo off the walls of your own helmet.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Thank you for feeding me things that didn\'92t come in vacuum-sealed sadness packets.\
I know the galley is limited. I know every meal is measured. But to me, each plate has felt like a holiday. Bread that bends. Fruit that is actually fruit. Coffee that tastes like something made with care. I will remember these meals long after the flavor fades.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Thank you for listening to the poem.\
It wasn\'92t just a poem \'97 it was a way of telling the truth without saying it directly. A way of recording Nemo so I could put it somewhere outside myself. I know two thousand words about dust and a rock named Gary isn\'92t everyone\'92s idea of a good time, but you listened anyway. You let me have that moment, and in doing so, you helped me keep a promise I\'92d made to myself: that I would leave Nemo having told its story.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Thank you for the quiet moments.\
Not the silence of Nemo \'97 which was deep and heavy and absolute \'97 but the comfortable kind that happens when you\'92re not alone, and nobody feels the need to fill the air with noise. Sitting in the observation deck, watching stars slide past, not saying anything because there\'92s nothing that needs to be said. That kind of silence is rare. You\'92ve given it to me here, and it\'92s been a balm.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Thank you for letting me feel useful again.\
Assigning me as \'93Light Morale Officer\'94 might have been a joke at first, but it\'92s meant more to me than you know. It\'92s reminded me that I can still contribute something \'97 that my presence here is more than just the subject of a rescue report. You\'92ve given me back a sense of worth I didn\'92t realize I\'92d misplaced on Nemo.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 And lastly \'97 thank you for coming at all.\
I know you have missions, priorities, schedules. I know a single stranded person isn\'92t always enough to justify the resources, the fuel, the risk. But you came. You crossed that void. You burned time and energy and focus on someone you\'92d never met, because you decided they mattered.\
You\'92ll never know the weight of that gift from my side of it. You\'92ve restored me to a life I thought I might never see again. You\'92ve carried me from dust to air, from silence to laughter, from survival to living.\
And I will spend the rest of my life telling people about you \'97 about how you appeared one day in my sky like a promise, and kept it.\
With gratitude deeper than the gravity well we\'92re falling toward,\uc0\u8232 \'97 
\f2\i [Name]
\f0\i0 \uc0\u8232 Nemo Survivor, Reluctant Poet, and Proud Passenger of the SS Relentless Hope\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
