\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 Reflection Essay \'96 \'93Between Dust and Air\'94
\f0\b0\fs24 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 When you\'92re stranded alone on an asteroid, the first thing you learn is that time stops caring about you.\uc0\u8232 It doesn\'92t speed up when you\'92re excited, it doesn\'92t slow down when you\'92re afraid \'97 it just marches forward with the same steady indifference it\'92s had since before you were born. I learned this on Nemo, in the long, stretched-out days when the only thing that moved faster than the sun was my own imagination.\
You might think a comedian stranded in space would find endless material. That\'92s what I thought too, for maybe the first week. After that, the punchlines started to feel stale. There\'92s only so much you can wring out of \'93a guy walks into a vacuum.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 The Nemo Mind\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 There\'92s a particular quiet that comes with a place like Nemo.\uc0\u8232 It\'92s not the polite quiet of a library, or the heavy quiet before a storm. It\'92s a deep, bone-level quiet, where even your thoughts sound louder than they should. Some days I would speak out loud just to hear another voice. Other days, I would stay silent for hours, because breaking that quiet felt like snapping a thin piece of glass.\
I filled the time with little rituals. Morning inspection of the habitat seals. Afternoon dust measurements. Evening coffee from a machine that hissed like it was tired of me. These weren\'92t 
\f2\i necessary
\f0\i0  every day \'97 they were just anchors, points to tie myself to so I wouldn\'92t drift in the sea of unmarked hours.\
And, of course, I had Gary. Starboard Gary. A rock. My audience, my co-star, my silent partner in survival. He never laughed at my jokes, but he also never interrupted my sets. In a way, that made him the best crowd I\'92d ever had.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 The Moment of Rescue\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 When the 
\f2\i SS Relentless Hope
\f0\i0  came into view, I thought it was a mirage. My brain had gotten very good at inventing things to look at in the sky. But then the comm crackled, and a voice \'97 warm, human, impossibly real \'97 asked if I could hear them.\
It\'92s hard to describe what it feels like to be seen again after months of being invisible. It\'92s like being underwater for just long enough that you forget the taste of air, and then someone pulls you up and you remember \'97 instantly, completely \'97 what you\'92ve been missing.\
The landing was gentle. The handshakes were firm. The air aboard their ship was 
\f2\i different
\f0\i0  \'97 richer somehow, as if every breath carried the weight of other people\'92s lives.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Life on the Relentless Hope\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 This ship has its own rhythm. Meals are at set times. Duties are rotated. There\'92s always the background hum of systems keeping us alive, a sound I didn\'92t have on Nemo. It\'92s a comfort and a distraction all at once.\
The crew is small, but larger than \'93just me,\'94 which was the entire population of my last home. I had to relearn how to have casual conversations \'97 the kind that aren\'92t about oxygen levels or ration counts. I had to remember that sometimes people just ask \'93How\'92s your day?\'94 because they 
\f2\i want to know
\f0\i0 , not because they\'92re checking for signs of mental collapse.\
And I had to get used to laughter again. Real laughter. It\'92s louder than you remember, and it\'92s addictive.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Between Two Worlds\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Here\'92s the strange part: I miss Nemo sometimes.\
I miss the predictability of it. The way my whole world fit into a few hundred meters of rock and habitat. I miss the ownership \'97 every system, every panel, every piece of gear, I knew like the back of my hand. I was responsible for all of it, and it was responsible for me.\
On this ship, I\'92m just one of many. I\'92m grateful for that \'97 believe me, I am \'97 but it\'92s different. My role is softer, less defined. I\'92m a passenger now, not the captain of my own tiny survival.\
And yet, when I think of Earth, I don\'92t think of feeling small. I think of feeling 
\f2\i overwhelmed
\f0\i0 .\
Crowds. Cities. Sky that goes up forever without the shelter of a dome. Smells and sounds I\'92ve forgotten. Choices \'97 infinite choices, about where to go, what to eat, who to be.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 The Comedian\'92s Truth\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Comedy, I\'92ve learned, is about contrast. The setup and the punchline. The familiar and the absurd. The truth and the lie you tell to make that truth sharper.\
My life right now is one long setup. Nemo was the premise: \'93A guy gets stuck alone on an asteroid.\'94 The rescue was the twist. This trip home is the long pause before the punchline lands.\
The funny thing is, I don\'92t know what that punchline will be yet. I don\'92t know if it\'92s going to be \'93And then he lived happily ever after\'94 or \'93And then he realized Earth wasn\'92t home anymore.\'94 Maybe it\'92ll be something in between \'97 something that makes the audience laugh and wince at the same time.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 Gratitude and Gravity\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 There\'92s one thing I\'92m certain of: I owe the people on this ship more than I can say. They didn\'92t just pull me off Nemo \'97 they\'92ve been carrying me through the slow, awkward process of becoming human again.\
They\'92ve let me fail at small talk. They\'92ve put up with my weird habits (I still talk to Gary). They\'92ve given me space when I needed it and company when I didn\'92t know I did.\
They\'92ve reminded me what it\'92s like to exist in a world \'97 even a small, metal one \'97 where you\'92re not the only heartbeat in the room.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa280\partightenfactor0

\f1\b\fs28 \cf0 What Comes Next\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 Three weeks from now, I\'92ll step off this ship and onto Earth soil. I\'92ll feel real wind. I\'92ll probably cry in public. I\'92ll see faces I haven\'92t seen in years.\
But I\'92ll also carry Nemo with me. I\'92ll carry the dust, the silence, the way the stars looked when there was nothing between me and them but vacuum. I\'92ll carry the version of myself that lived there \'97 the one who learned patience, who learned how to be alone without breaking, who learned to make a friend out of a rock and mean it.\
And maybe that\'92s the real punchline: you can take the comedian off the asteroid, but you can\'92t take the asteroid out of the comedian.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0
\cf0 That\'92s where I am now \'97 between dust and air. Not quite home, not quite gone. Waiting for the moment when the setup meets the punchline, and hoping the laugh, when it comes, will be worth the long pause.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa298\partightenfactor0

