\f1\b\fs36 \cf0 Week 24 \'96 \'93The Last Page of the Nemo Diaries\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\f0\b0\fs24 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Day 1 \'96 The Countdown
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 I woke up to the smell of coffee and the sound of seagulls. Not real seagulls \'97 a sound loop on my phone \'97 but still, after months of space hum and mechanical air pumps, it\'92s oddly exotic. This week, I promised myself I\'92d write my last log. No more day-by-day survival notes, no more Gary check-ins, no more arguments with myself over whether eating beans three days in a row constitutes a \'93diet.\'94\
This morning, I caught myself checking the oxygen monitor on my wall. Not because I needed to. Out of habit. And that\'92s when it hit me: a part of me is still on Nemo.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Day 2 \'96 The Media Goodbye Tour
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 StarHorizon\'92s offer is still in limbo (my last letter might have delayed things by another decade), but journalists have already moved on to the next sensation: a guy in Montana who claims to have built a wormhole in his garage. I gave my last interview today. The anchor asked, \'93What\'92s next for you?\'94\
I said, \'93Laundry.\'94\uc0\u8232 It\'92s true. My actual, literal laundry has been threatening to start a sentient colony in my closet.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Day 3 \'96 Gary\'92s Departure
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 Gary is going to a museum in Italy. My choice.\u8232 We had one last coffee together. I put him on the table, told him about my plans to finally start a podcast, maybe write a book. He said nothing. But I swear his surface looked more reflective, like he was listening harder than usual.\
When the museum curator took him away, I thought I\'92d feel relieved. Instead, it felt like giving away a diary. But Gary\'92s story isn\'92t ending. He\'92ll sit behind glass, and people will press their noses to see him. And maybe, just maybe, some kid will decide to become a space explorer because of him.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Day 4 \'96 Nemo Dreams
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 I had a dream I was back there \'97 inside the dome, with beans boiling and the dust swirling outside like an orange snowstorm. In the dream, I wasn\'92t scared or lonely. I was\'85 home.\
Woke up wondering if that\'92s what astronauts feel when they look at the Moon years later \'97 not remembering the fear, just the stillness.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Day 5 \'96 The Official Goodbye
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 Today, I stood on the beach, toes in wet sand, recording my final Nemo log. The ocean looked infinite, just like the view from the dome, but louder, wetter, saltier. I talked into the mic for 45 minutes. About the silence. About the humor. About how survival is mostly patience with a side of stubbornness.\
Then I said the words:\uc0\u8232 \'93This is the last log. Nemo out.\'94\
And I stopped recording.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Day 6 \'96 Letting Go of the Schedule
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 It\'92s strange \'97 on Nemo, every day was logged, counted, measured. Back on Earth, days are just days. The end of the log means letting them blend together again. No more marking oxygen levels or bean supplies. No more playing chess with myself (I\'92m a terrible opponent).\
I deleted the alarm labeled \'93Water Reclaimer Check.\'94 It felt like deleting an old contact from my phone \'97 necessary but sad.\
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\b \cf0 Day 7 \'96 Moving On
\f0\b0 \uc0\u8232 I went for a walk. No helmet, no suit. Just me, air, and a city that smells like bread and exhaust. I passed people talking on phones, arguing about groceries, laughing over coffee. None of them know the sound an asteroid\'92s night makes, or how quiet a rock can be.\
I think that\'92s okay. Nemo was mine. Gary was mine. And now, they belong to history.\
As for me \'97 I\'92m done logging.\uc0\u8232 Next week, I\'92ll be just another man with a story.\
But tonight, I\'92m sitting here with my last cup of coffee, watching the sun set. It\'92s big and bright and so warm it almost feels like home is looking back at me, saying, \'93You made it.\'94}